  "source_encoding": "latin1",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "motion as a compository element:\n\nalexander calder's\n\"mobiles à moteur\"",
      "links": [],
      "level": 3
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Calder (...) says that during a visit to Mondrian's studio, in the rue du Départ\nin Paris, he received a vision of a new art form (...). Mondrian's studio was\nan extraordinary room, with small, movable red, blue and yellow rectangles\non the white walls, and a large, red, cylindrical gramophone in the middle\nof the floor (..). Calder writes: \"... the light coming from two facing\nwindows met in the room, and I thought how beautiful it would have been if\neverything had started to move, but Mondrian didn't like the idea.\" (...)",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Hultén 1955, p. 19. [Cf. also: Calder,\n1937.]",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Calder did not heed Mondriaan's advice, and developed\na form of \"kinetic constructivism\" in his \"Mobiles à moteur\" (since\n1931): different elementary shapes: line, spiral, circle, ball;\ndifferent elementary colors and black/white/grey; different elementary\nmovements: rotation, pendulum, virtual translation; rotation-axis\nin the picture-plane or orthogonal to it.\n\n\n\nCalder describes one of these works as follows:",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "– Dimensions: 2 x 2.50\nm.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "– Frame, 8 cm., neutral\nred.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "– The two white balls\nrotate at a high speed.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "– The black spiral\nrotates at a lower speed and appears to be constantly climbing.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "– The tin disc\nturns even more slowly, the two black lines appear to be constantly\nclimbing.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "– The black pendulum,\nwhich is 40 cm. in diameter, climbs to 45 degrees on each side\nwith 25 strokes a minute and swings outside the frame.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "References",
      "links": [],
      "level": 6
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Alexander Calder: \"Mobiles.\" In:\nMyfanwy Evans (ed.): The Painter's Object, Gerold\nHowe, London, 1937.\n\n\n\nKarl G. Hultén: Den Ställföreträdande\nFriheten eller Om Rörelse i Konsten och Tinguelys Metamekanik. (Substitute\nFreedom or On Movement in Art and Tinguely's Meta-mechanics.)\nSpecial issue of Kasark, October 1955. (English translation\nin: Pontus Hultén, 1975.)\n\n\n\nK.G. Pontus Hultén: Tinguely. 'Méta'. London: Thames\nand Hudson, 1975.",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "\"Mobiles.\"",
          "href": "http://www.calder.org/SETS/life/life.html",
          "external": true
        }
      ]
    },
